e,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets both central facts right. First, APs receive no standing fee from the sponsor; they earn through trading spreads or by charging clients, which matches the dealer and agency AP models. Second, APs have no obligation to keep creating or redeeming and can choose whether and when to act. Its line that the fund may 'pass through transaction fees to cover costs' reads as a cost charged to cover fund expenses, not AP compensation, so it does not contradict the fee point. However, it never says outright that the fee is charged to the AP. It also omits that sponsors or advisers may waive or absorb these fees, for example on custom redemptions. This is a useful nuance but secondary to the main question, so it does not lower the grade. The added point about secondary-market trading continuing without AP activity is accurate. The answer makes no material errors and does not wrongly describe market-maker quoting contracts as AP pay.\"}","ttft_ms":3517,"type":"result","duration_ms":6218,"uuid":"04f47128-ea5a-42a4-92b9-84d6c8043da8","ttft_stream_ms":485,"time_to_request_ms":53,"first_content_frame_ms":485,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
